  "note": "In-sample, one AI-bull regime. We govern DELTA (net beta ~0); leverage of a delta-neutral book PRESERVES Sharpe (~3.3-3.4 across the frontier) \u2014 return scales ~linearly with gross. Face net-notional rises with gross (a SOXL-3x artifact \u2014 a small hedge face delivers a large delta hedge; we stay delta-flat). DD on the engine's trading-notional basis (10% notional = 20% of strategy capital, the risk-policy hard stop). WHY DD STOPS RISING WITH LEVERAGE: not the DD ladder. The unbraked column (ladder + hard stop off) is IDENTICAL to the braked one, because run_paper only consults the ladder at the 21-day rebalance and so never sees an intra-month drawdown \u2014 over the whole sample it fired one rung, once. It is the 200% GROSS CAP that clips the book on its largest days, which caps realised vol and therefore caps drawdown; the displayed gross is a final-day snapshot and hides that the cap was binding elsewhere in the sample. Lift the cap at an 18% vol target and the same book realises 30.3% NAV vol and -23.8% NAV drawdown, THROUGH the hard stop, at an unchanged Sharpe. So read dd_*_uncapped as the drawdown the strategy generates and the braked column as what the cap left of it, and do not read rising Calmar as evidence that leverage is free. Two caveats on the ladder itself: live_run DOES re-check it every daily tick, so the live book is better protected than any number here shows, and equally no backtest has ever exercised it \u2014 and any brake assumes drawdowns arrive slowly enough to de-gross into, which a gap (earnings, policy shock, perp dislocation) would not honour."
